# Good Morning Good Morning, tune phrases spanning a fourth split into a third and a second
# (figure 116.1)
# Pollack's list of examples, each phrase one system named by its words, its notes in equal
# shares (he gives pitches only). Octaves chosen so each phrase spans the fourth he describes.
#? Octaves are a guess from the record (E4 up to D5); "To save his life" (E G A E) in particular may sit an octave higher.
key A

system Nothing to do
voice   | A4 F#4 B4 |

system To save his life
voice   | E4 G4 A4 E4 |

system Call his wife in
voice   | G4 A4 F#4 A4 |

system I've got nothing
voice   | D5 C#5 A4 A4 |

system (nothing) to say
voice   | A4 A4 F#4 B4 |

system Everybody knows
voice   | C#5 C#5 C#5 A4 D5 |

system Good morning
voice   | A4 F#4 E4 |
